-- title:
--   Lattices of a Cartier quadruple at a ξ-point
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\iota : W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to W(k)$ a ring homomorphism (`Zp2 p` being $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$). For a commutative ring $B$, `pIdeal p B` is the ideal generated by $p$; write $\bar\jmath = \iota$ followed by the quotient map $W(k) \to W(k)/pW(k)$.
--
--   The first block of data is a formal $O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $W(k)/pW(k)$, that is, a two-dimensional commutative formal group law together with an action of $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi \circ \varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi \circ [a] = [\sigma a] \circ \varpi$, subject to: `hΦ`, that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\jmath$ (the weight-$0$ and weight-$1$ parts of its Lie module are complementary and each invertible); `hΦ4`, that the kernel of $[p]$ on $\Phi$ has degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, that the weight-$0$ part of the Lie module is contained in the kernel of the linear part of $\varpi$; and `hcΦ`, that the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ (the subgroups on which the Teichmüller action of $c \in \mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ is homothety by $\bar\jmath(\tau(c))^{p^n}$, $n = 0, 1$) are complementary. On the resulting graded Cartier module data, `rΦ` is an additive map from $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ to the associated module `NMod`, and `hrΦ` requires that for every canonical $L$-map $L$ on these data, `rΦ` maps all of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ $\eta$-piece $\mathrm{etaPiece}\,L\,0$; together $(\mathtt{hc\Phi}, \mathtt{r\Phi}, \mathtt{hr\Phi})$ constitute the rigidification of $\Phi$. Further, $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ is the element with matrix $\mathrm{diag}(p, 1)$ (`hg`).
--
--   The second block is the edge family. Let $E$ denote the ring `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p (W(k)/pW(k))`, an algebra over $W(k)/pW(k)$ with distinguished elements $\xi, \eta$, and let $X$ be a formal $O_D$-module over $E$ with $\gamma : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathcal{M}(X)$ a pair of elements of its Cartier module such that: `hγ`, $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure map $\iota$ followed by $W(k) \to W(k)/pW(k) \to E$, i.e. $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece of index $i$ and the matrix of tangent vectors of the $\gamma_i$ has unit determinant; `hγa`, $X$ has structure constants the edge constants `edgeRingConstants p (W(k)/pW(k))` formed from $\xi$ and $\eta$, i.e. for each $i$ and each $N$ the element $\varpi \gamma_i$ agrees with $\sum_{m<N} V^m(\,a_{m,i} \cdot \gamma_{\mathrm{piIndex}(m,i)}\,)$ modulo $V^N$; `hXs`, $X$ is special for that structure map; and `hX4`, $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   The third block is the node point and the node isogeny: a ring homomorphism $f_0 : E \to W(k)/pW(k)$ splitting the structure map (`hf₀`) and annihilating both $\xi$ (`hf₀ξ`) and $\eta$ (`hf₀η`); a natural number $m$ and a two-variable power series datum $\rho_0$ over $W(k)/pW(k)$ which, by `hρ₀`, is an isogeny $\Phi \to f_{0*}X$ of formal $O_D$-modules whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Let $t_0$ be the rigidified object $(f_{0*}X,\, m,\, \rho_0 \bmod p)$ over $W(k)/pW(k)$. The grading data for $t_0$ along the identity of $W(k)/pW(k)$ are: `hOD₀`, that the reduction $\rho$ of $t_0$ is a homomorphism of formal $O_D$-modules from the reduction of $\Phi$ to that of $f_{0*}X$; `hcb`, complementarity of the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ for the reduced $X$-side of $t_0$; and `hcΦg`, the same complementarity on the $\Phi$-side.
--
--   The normalisation hypothesis consists of a natural number $a$ and `hN`, which states that for both $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$ one has
--   $$p^a \cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(\iota, \mathtt{hc\Phi}, \mathtt{r\Phi}, t_0, \mathtt{hOD_0}, \mathrm{id}, \mathtt{hcb}, \mathtt{hc\Phi g})(e_i) = p^{a+m} \cdot v_i,$$
--   where $\mathrm{rigidNum}$ is the composite of `rΦ` with the two transition maps on `NMod` induced by base change and by the reduction of $t_0$, $e_i$ is the $i$-th standard vector of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, and $v_0 = \mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_0, 0)$, $v_1 = \mathrm{nVarpi}\,\mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_1, 0)$, with $\bar\gamma_i$ the image of $\gamma_i$ under base change along $f_0$ followed by the two reduction maps.
--
--   The fourth block is the edge isogeny: a homomorphism $\rho_1$ of formal $O_D$-modules over $E$ from the base change of $f_{0*}X$ to $E$ into $X$, such that on Cartier modules (`hρ₁0`, `hρ₁1`)
--   $$\rho_1(\gamma_0) = p\,\gamma_0 - V\big(\tau(\eta^{p^2})\,\gamma_1\big), \qquad \rho_1(\gamma_1) = p\,\gamma_1 - V\big(\tau(\xi^{p^2})\,\gamma_0\big),$$
--   where the arguments on the left are the images of $\gamma_0, \gamma_1$ under base change along $f_0$ and then along $W(k)/pW(k) \to E$, $\tau$ is the Teichmüller map into $W(E)$ and $V$ is the integral Verschiebung; and `hρ₁h`, that $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$.
--
--   Finally, $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, $y : E \to \Omega$ a ring homomorphism with $y(\xi) \neq 0$ (`hyξ`), $Q$ a Drinfeld datum over $\Omega$ for the uniformiser $p \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ with $K = \mathbb{Q}_p$ (a pair of full $\mathbb{Z}_p$-lattices $N_0(x) \le N_1(x)$ in $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$ for each $x \in \operatorname{Spec}\Omega$ with $p\,N_1(x) \subseteq N_0(x)$ and open membership loci, together with invertible $\Omega$-modules $T_0, T_1$, maps $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ composing to multiplication by $p$, and comparison maps $u_0, u_1$ on stalks), and `hQ` the hypothesis that $Q$ is a Cartier quadruple, in the sense of the predicate `IsCartierQuadruple`, for the base change along $y$ of the rigidified object $t = (X,\, m+1,\, \bar\rho_1 \circ (\rho_0 \text{ base changed to } E))$, with respect to $\iota$, `hcΦ`, `rΦ` and the structure map $\iota$ followed by $W(k) \to W(k)/pW(k) \to E \xrightarrow{y} \Omega$; the clauses of that predicate — that the reduced $\rho$ is a homomorphism of formal $O_D$-modules, that $T_0$ and $T_1$ are identified with the weight-$0$ and weight-$1$ parts of the Lie module of the $X$-component in a way interchanged by the linear part of $\varpi$ and by $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$, and that at every prime the lattices $N_0$, $N_1$ are cut out vectorwise by $\eta$-sections of the graded Cartier module data over localisations, compatibly with $u_0$ and $u_1$ — are summarised here. Let $x$ be a point of $\operatorname{Spec}\Omega$.
--
--   The conclusion is a conjunction of two equalities of $\mathbb{Z}_p$-submodules of $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$: first, $N_0(x)$ is the standard lattice $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, the underlying submodule of `stdFullLattice`; and second, $N_1(x)$ is the underlying submodule of the translate of `stdFullLattice` by the scalar matrix $\mathrm{scalarGL}$ of the inverse of the unit of $\mathbb{Q}_p$ attached to $p \neq 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}_p$, i.e. $N_1(x) = p^{-1}\mathbb{Z}_p^2$.
--
--   This is the lattice half of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison at an interior point of the $\xi$-branch of the rigidified edge family: there the pair of lattices attached to any Cartier quadruple is forced to be the standard vertex pair $(\mathbb{Z}_p^2,\,p^{-1}\mathbb{Z}_p^2)$, so that the associated point of the Drinfeld space is the one prescribed by the edge chart. It is used in [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_edge_isogeny_of_apply_xi_ne_zero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_edge_isogeny_of_apply_xi_ne_zero), which assembles the quadruple at such a point together with the identification of its line.
